__d(function(e,i,t,a,o,n,s){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:22,chapter_title:"Diversity and Discrimination",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is discrimination? How is it different from diversity?",back:"Discrimination is the unfair or unequal treatment of a person or group based on their identity \u2014 such as caste, religion, gender, language, or disability \u2014 rather than on their individual merit or actions. Diversity simply describes the existence of differences among people. Discrimination is what happens when people respond to diversity with prejudice and unequal treatment. Example: refusing to hire a qualified person because of their religion is discrimination. Diversity itself is neutral \u2014 discrimination is the unjust response to it. India's Constitution explicitly prohibits discrimination.",memory_hook:"DISCRIMINATE = SEPARATE UNFAIRLY. Like sorting good apples from bad \u2014 but instead of quality, you are sorting by caste or religion. The apple has nothing to do with quality \u2014 the sorting is wrong."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the caste system? How did it lead to discrimination in India?",back:"The caste system is a hierarchical social system in India where people were born into a caste (jati) that determined their occupation, social status, and whom they could marry, eat with, or live near. Historically, the four varnas were: Brahmin (priests/scholars), Kshatriya (warriors/rulers), Vaishya (traders), Shudra (cultivators/servants). Below these were communities considered 'untouchable' (now called Dalits) who were assigned the most degrading work. Caste discrimination meant: Dalits were denied access to wells, temples, schools, and public spaces; they were forced into hereditary occupations regardless of ability; they faced violence if they violated caste norms. The Constitution abolished untouchability (Article 17).",memory_hook:"CASTE = BIRTH SENTENCE. Born into a caste \u2192 occupation decided, neighbourhood decided, life decided \u2014 regardless of your talent or choice. The Constitution said: BIRTH CANNOT DECIDE WORTH."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who were referred to as 'untouchables'? What is the preferred term today and why?",back:"In the caste system, communities assigned the most degrading labour (removing dead animals, manual scavenging, leatherwork) were considered 'untouchable' \u2014 meaning contact with them was believed to 'pollute' upper-caste people. They were denied access to wells, temples, schools, and public roads. Today the preferred terms are: Dalit (meaning 'oppressed' or 'broken' \u2014 a term chosen by these communities themselves as an assertion of identity and dignity) or Scheduled Castes (the official constitutional/legal term). 'Untouchable' is rejected because it affirms the discriminatory logic it was born from. B.R. Ambedkar \u2014 himself a Dalit \u2014 fought his entire life against this discrimination.",memory_hook:"DALIT = CHOSEN NAME OF DIGNITY. Communities don't want a name given by oppressors. 'Dalit' was chosen by the community. Like Blacks in the US choosing 'African American' \u2014 naming yourself is an act of power."},{id:"fc04",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How did B.R. Ambedkar experience caste discrimination? What did he do about it?",back:"B.R. Ambedkar (1891\u20131956) was born into the Mahar caste (considered untouchable in Maharashtra). His experiences of discrimination: (1) As a child, he was made to sit separately in class and was not allowed to drink from the school water pot; (2) He had to carry his own mat to school as Dalit children weren't allowed to sit on the floor used by others; (3) On a journey, no one would serve him water and he couldn't get a tonga (carriage) driver to take him. Despite this, he became one of India's most educated men (multiple doctorates from Columbia and LSE). His responses: wrote extensively against caste; led the Mahad Satyagraha (1927) asserting the right to drink from a public tank; chaired the committee that drafted India's Constitution; had Article 17 (abolition of untouchability) included.",memory_hook:"AMBEDKAR = PROOF THAT DISCRIMINATION CANNOT STOP DETERMINATION. Born 'untouchable,' became the architect of the Constitution. His life is the greatest argument against caste discrimination."},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does Article 17 of the Indian Constitution state?",back:"Article 17 of the Indian Constitution states: 'Untouchability is abolished and its practice in any form is forbidden. The enforcement of any disability arising out of Untouchability shall be an offence punishable in accordance with law.' This means: (1) No person can be treated as untouchable; (2) Denying access to temples, public places, water sources, or services on grounds of untouchability is a criminal offence; (3) The Protection of Civil Rights Act (1955) and the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act (1989) provide specific legal penalties for such discrimination. The Constitution didn't just disapprove of untouchability \u2014 it made it illegal.",memory_hook:"ARTICLE 17 = THE CONSTITUTION'S SWORD AGAINST UNTOUCHABILITY. Not just 'wrong' \u2014 ILLEGAL. Punishable. The Constitution gave the oppressed a legal weapon. Know this article \u2014 it is one of India's most important."},{id:"fc06",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is gender discrimination? Give three examples from Indian society.",back:"Gender discrimination is the unequal treatment of people based on their gender (male, female, or others). Examples in India: (1) Son preference \u2014 families celebrate a son's birth more than a daughter's; sex-selective abortion (illegal) has skewed India's sex ratio; (2) Education \u2014 in some communities, girls are pulled out of school earlier than boys, or not sent at all; (3) Work \u2014 women are often paid less than men for the same work (gender pay gap); (4) Household responsibility \u2014 women are expected to do all housework without pay, limiting their professional advancement; (5) Safety \u2014 women face harassment in public spaces, limiting their movement and opportunities. Gender discrimination is not biological \u2014 it is socially constructed and reinforced by custom, not nature.",memory_hook:"GENDER DISCRIMINATION = TREATING HALF OF HUMANITY AS LESS. Not based on ability (women prove equal or superior in every field) but on tradition and prejudice. The Constitution says: EQUALITY REGARDLESS OF SEX (Article 15)."},{id:"fc07",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is the practice of untouchability considered a violation of human dignity?",back:"Untouchability violates human dignity because: (1) It treats human beings as physically 'polluting' based solely on birth \u2014 denying them basic humanity; (2) It forces people into degrading, dangerous occupations (manual scavenging) without choice; (3) It denies equal access to public goods \u2014 water, temples, roads, schools \u2014 paid for by all citizens' taxes; (4) It instils shame and self-hatred in children who have done nothing wrong; (5) It makes a person's worth depend entirely on birth, denying individual agency and potential. Every human being \u2014 regardless of birth \u2014 has inherent dignity. The Constitution's Preamble declares India is committed to 'fraternity assuring the dignity of the individual' \u2014 untouchability is the direct opposite of this commitment.",memory_hook:"DIGNITY = HUMAN BIRTHRIGHT. Every person is born with dignity \u2014 no caste system, no tradition, no religious text can legitimately take it away. What you are born into cannot determine your worth as a human being."},{id:"fc08",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Mahad Satyagraha of 1927? Why was it significant?",back:"The Mahad Satyagraha (1927) was led by B.R. Ambedkar in Mahad, Maharashtra. A public tank (Chavadar Tank) in Mahad had been technically opened to all citizens by the local council in 1924, but Dalits were still prevented from using it by upper-caste violence and social pressure. Ambedkar led thousands of Dalit men and women to the tank to assert their right to drink water from a public source. Significance: (1) It was the first organised assertion by Dalits of their right to public spaces and resources; (2) It challenged the idea that caste discrimination was natural or acceptable; (3) It established that access to public resources (water, roads, temples) cannot be caste-restricted; (4) It preceded the independence movement, showing that social equality was as important as political freedom.",memory_hook:"MAHAD SATYAGRAHA = WATER IS A RIGHT, NOT A PRIVILEGE. The most basic human need \u2014 water \u2014 was being denied based on caste. Ambedkar said: if independence means anything, it means the right to drink from a public tank. A simple act of drinking water became a revolution."},{id:"fc09",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How is religious discrimination different from having a religion?",back:"Having a religion means personally practising a faith \u2014 attending prayers, following customs, celebrating festivals. This is a fundamental right (Article 25 of the Constitution). Religious discrimination means treating people unequally or denying them rights because of their religion \u2014 for example, refusing to rent a house to someone because of their religion; communal riots targeting a minority; writing off a person's qualifications because they belong to a religious minority. One is a private choice; the other is a public injustice. A secular state like India protects every citizen's right to practice their religion AND protects every citizen from discrimination based on religion. Both protections are essential.",memory_hook:"RELIGION = YOUR PRIVATE RIGHT (protected by Article 25). RELIGIOUS DISCRIMINATION = PUBLIC WRONG (prohibited by Article 15). The state protects your right to believe AND your right not to be punished for your beliefs."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act? Why was it passed?",back:"The Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989 is an Indian law that provides specific legal protection to Dalits (Scheduled Castes) and Adivasis (Scheduled Tribes) from discrimination, violence, and atrocities. It was passed because: despite Article 17 abolishing untouchability, violence and discrimination against Dalits and Adivasis continued; existing laws were inadequate to deal with caste-based atrocities. The Act makes specific caste-based crimes (forcing someone to eat inedible substances, parading them naked, denying access to water, land grabbing) punishable offences. It provides for special courts and rehabilitation of victims. It recognises that Dalits and Adivasis face unique vulnerabilities requiring specific legal protection.",memory_hook:"SC/ST ATROCITIES ACT = THE CONSTITUTION'S SHIELD MADE INTO A SWORD. Not enough to say discrimination is wrong \u2014 the law must punish it. 1989 Act gives teeth to Article 17's promise."},{id:"fc11",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does discrimination affect the person being discriminated against?",back:"Discrimination affects its targets in multiple ways: (1) Educational \u2014 denied access to quality schools or encouraged to drop out early \u2192 limits future opportunities; (2) Economic \u2014 denied jobs, promotions, or fair wages based on identity \u2192 perpetuates poverty; (3) Psychological \u2014 constant experience of being treated as inferior causes low self-esteem, depression, and shame; children internalise the message that they are less worthy; (4) Physical \u2014 caste or religious violence causes injury, displacement, and death; (5) Social \u2014 exclusion from public spaces (temples, water sources, restaurants) isolates communities; (6) Political \u2014 historically denied voting rights or political representation. Discrimination creates a cycle: inequality \u2192 discrimination \u2192 more inequality. Breaking this cycle requires both legal protection and social change.",memory_hook:"DISCRIMINATION = COMPOUND INTEREST ON INJUSTICE. It doesn't just hurt once \u2014 it compounds. One generation denied education \u2192 next generation born into poverty \u2192 next generation also denied education. The cycle must be consciously broken."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are some ways to fight discrimination in daily life?",back:"Fighting discrimination in daily life: (1) Refuse to use casteist, religious, or gendered slurs \u2014 words normalise discrimination; (2) Speak up when you witness discriminatory jokes or comments \u2014 silence is complicity; (3) Treat all people with equal dignity regardless of their background \u2014 in school, neighbourhood, workplace; (4) Question your own assumptions \u2014 if you find yourself making assumptions about people based on their identity, stop and reflect; (5) Learn about the history and culture of communities different from yours \u2014 knowledge builds empathy; (6) Support legal mechanisms \u2014 report discrimination when you see it; (7) Vote for leaders who uphold equality. Change happens at two levels: individual (attitudes) and structural (laws and institutions). Both are necessary.",memory_hook:"3 LEVELS OF CHANGE: THINK (unlearn stereotypes), SPEAK (challenge discrimination when heard), ACT (treat everyone with dignity). You have control over all three. Start with what you can control today."},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between equality in law and equality in practice?",back:"Equality in law means that the Constitution and legislation give every person equal rights \u2014 no discrimination before the law. India's Constitution provides this: Article 14 (equality before law), Article 15 (no discrimination based on religion/race/caste/sex/birthplace), Article 17 (abolition of untouchability). Equality in practice means that in real daily life, people actually experience equal treatment. India has legal equality but unequal practice \u2014 Dalits may legally have the right to enter a temple but still face violence if they try; women have equal voting rights but face domestic violence; tribal people have Constitutional protection but still lose land to corporations. The gap between legal equality and lived equality is the central challenge. Closing this gap requires implementation, education, and sustained social change.",memory_hook:"LAW = WRITTEN PROMISE. PRACTICE = KEPT PROMISE. India wrote the promise in 1950 (Constitution). We are still working to keep it in 2025. The gap between the written promise and the kept promise = India's ongoing work in progress."},{id:"fc14",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How did Kuffir and the story in the NCERT chapter illustrate the experience of caste discrimination?",back:"The NCERT chapter uses personal narratives \u2014 including that of Surekha (a Dalit woman from Maharashtra in the textbook) \u2014 to illustrate how discrimination is experienced as a lived reality, not just an abstract wrong. Her account shows: (1) Discrimination happens in ordinary, everyday moments \u2014 at school, at the water tap, on the road; (2) It is experienced from childhood \u2014 the shame and exclusion are imprinted early; (3) It persists despite laws \u2014 untouchability was abolished in 1950, but Surekha's experiences are from decades later; (4) It affects the whole family \u2014 parents feel helpless watching their children face what they faced. The personal narrative format is used deliberately \u2014 statistics about discrimination are less powerful than one person's story. Human dignity is understood through human experience.",memory_hook:"ONE STORY = ONE THOUSAND STATISTICS. Reading Surekha's account is more powerful than reading that X% of Dalits face discrimination. Numbers abstract. Stories humanise. That is why the textbook uses personal accounts."},{id:"fc15",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name two social reformers who fought against caste discrimination in India.",back:"Social reformers against caste discrimination: (1) B.R. Ambedkar (1891\u20131956) \u2014 Dalit leader, jurist, economist, led Mahad Satyagraha; drafted India's Constitution; included Article 17 abolishing untouchability; later converted to Buddhism as a rejection of the caste system. (2) Mahatma Gandhi \u2014 used the term 'Harijan' (children of God) for Dalits; campaigned against untouchability; insisted on cleaning toilets himself as a symbolic act against the degradation of Dalits. (3) Jyotirao Phule (1827\u20131890) \u2014 Maharashtra; opened schools for girls and lower castes; wrote Gulamgiri (Slavery) exposing caste oppression; (4) Periyar E.V. Ramasamy \u2014 Tamil Nadu; championed self-respect movement against Brahmin dominance; (5) Basavanna \u2014 12th century Karnataka; rejected caste in his Vachana movement.",memory_hook:"BAAPJ: Basavanna, Ambedkar, (Gandhi), Phule, (Periyar) + Jyotirao. Five pillars of India's anti-caste tradition across five centuries. Know at least Ambedkar and one other for the exam."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'intersectionality' mean in the context of discrimination?",back:"Intersectionality means that a person can face multiple forms of discrimination simultaneously because of overlapping identities. A Dalit woman, for example, faces both caste discrimination AND gender discrimination \u2014 her experience is worse than that of either a Dalit man or an upper-caste woman alone. Similarly, a Dalit Muslim faces caste discrimination within the Muslim community AND religious discrimination from Hindu society. A tribal woman faces gender, caste (tribal status), and sometimes regional discrimination together. Understanding intersectionality means recognising that discrimination is not one-dimensional \u2014 the most vulnerable people often face multiple layers of oppression simultaneously, and solutions must address all layers.",memory_hook:"INTERSECTIONALITY = WHERE OPPRESSIONS MEET AND MULTIPLY. Caste + gender = double discrimination. Caste + religion + poverty = triple. The person at the intersection of multiple marginalised identities faces the sharpest discrimination. Simple solutions won't work for complex overlapping problems."},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does Karnataka's history of Dalit movements and social reform reflect the chapter's themes?",back:"Karnataka's Dalit movements: (1) Ambedkar's influence \u2014 Karnataka has a strong Dalit Buddhist movement following Ambedkar's 1956 conversion; the blue Ambedkarite flag is prominent in Dalit neighbourhoods across Bengaluru; (2) Dalit Sangharsha Samiti (DSS) \u2014 Karnataka-based organisation founded in 1974, fighting for Dalit rights, land redistribution, and against atrocities; (3) Devaraj Urs government (1972\u201377) \u2014 as Chief Minister, implemented land reforms benefiting Dalits and OBCs; (4) The Vachana tradition \u2014 Basavanna's 12th century poetry explicitly rejected caste hierarchy ('The low-born who devotes his mind to the divine is nobler than a Brahmin who doesn't') \u2014 an ancient Karnataka voice against discrimination; (5) B. Krishnappa \u2014 first Dalit Chief Minister of Karnataka (1983, briefly).",memory_hook:"Karnataka's anti-discrimination story: Basavanna (12th c.) \u2192 Ambedkarite movement (20th c.) \u2192 DSS (1974) \u2192 land reforms (Devaraj Urs). Each generation renewed the fight. The fight is ongoing."},{id:"fc18",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why did Dr. Ambedkar convert to Buddhism? What message did it send about caste discrimination?",back:"On October 14, 1956, just weeks before his death, B.R. Ambedkar led approximately 600,000 Dalits in a mass conversion to Buddhism in Nagpur. His reasons: (1) He had studied all major religions and found Buddhism to be based on reason, equality, and compassion \u2014 free from caste hierarchy; (2) He rejected Hinduism as practiced because the caste system was legitimised by it; (3) Conversion was a political statement \u2014 'I will not die a Hindu' \u2014 asserting that Dalits had the agency to leave a system that oppressed them; (4) Buddhism was India's own tradition \u2014 the Buddha rejected caste and taught human equality; choosing Buddhism was choosing Indian roots without caste. The message: Dignity is non-negotiable. When a system denies your humanity, you have the right \u2014 and obligation \u2014 to reject it.",memory_hook:"AMBEDKAR'S CONVERSION = ULTIMATE ACT OF SELF-LIBERATION. You cannot reform a system from within if it has decided you are subhuman. Sometimes you have to walk out. His conversion was the loudest possible rejection of caste discrimination."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the mid-day meal scheme in Indian schools address caste and class discrimination?",back:"The Mid-Day Meal Scheme (now PM POSHAN) provides free cooked meals to children in government schools. Its anti-discrimination effects: (1) All children eat the same food together \u2014 breaking caste-based segregation at mealtimes; historically, Dalit children were made to sit separately or bring their own plates; (2) It ensures children from poor families get at least one nutritious meal \u2014 reducing hunger-based absenteeism; (3) It attracts children from marginalised families to school \u2014 improving enrolment of Dalit, tribal, and girl children; (4) Shared meals create social bonds across caste and class barriers; (5) Children who cook and eat together build familiarity that challenges inherited prejudices. A simple meal becomes a powerful tool against discrimination \u2014 equality practiced daily at school tables.",memory_hook:"SAME PLATE, SAME FOOD, SAME TABLE = EQUALITY IN ACTION. The mid-day meal does more than feed bodies \u2014 it feeds the idea that every child is equal. Daily practice of equality is how you dismantle centuries of hierarchy."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is 'manual scavenging'? Why is it considered both discrimination and a violation of human dignity?",back:"Manual scavenging is the practice of manually cleaning dry latrines, sewers, and septic tanks without protective equipment. It was (and in some areas still is) hereditary \u2014 meaning certain Dalit communities were assigned this work by caste for generations, passed from parent to child regardless of desire. Why it is discrimination: (1) It was assigned by birth (caste) not by choice; (2) It was paid poorly or not at all; (3) It exposed workers to toxic gases, bacteria, and physical danger \u2014 many die annually in sewers; (4) Those who did this work were then considered 'polluted' and excluded from normal social life \u2014 a double injustice. The Prohibition of Employment as Manual Scavengers and their Rehabilitation Act (2013) legally bans it. But practice continues in parts of India. Karnataka has manual scavenging rehabilitation programmes through the Social Welfare Department.",memory_hook:"MANUAL SCAVENGING = CASTE DISCRIMINATION IN CONCRETE FORM. Born into a caste \u2192 assigned the most dangerous, degrading work \u2192 called 'impure' because of that work \u2192 excluded from society. The circle of oppression made visible. 2013 Act says: ILLEGAL. Yet it continues. That gap = India's ongoing challenge."}]}},2931,[]);